# What does this service know about every endpoint? One row per endpoint per
# metric, over the whole registry: the endpoint, the metric id, the dqv:value
# verdict where the run measured it, and the sw:notMeasuredReason where the run
# declined it instead. Plus, per row and constant within a run, the run these
# facts came from and whether it finished, and the newest run in the store and
# whether IT finished and reached this endpoint.
#
# This is web/queries/endpoint_measurements.rq asked of every endpoint at once,
# and it is deliberately the same query shape: the index and the endpoint page
# are two renderings of one question, and a reader that selected its run
# differently could show a verdict the endpoint's own page contradicts. So the
# per-endpoint pointer, the one hop to the run graph, the type patterns and the
# newest-run branch are all the shapes that file argues for, for the reasons it
# gives, and every column it selects except the substituted ?endpoint is
# selected here too.
#
# ?level and ?elapsedMs are here even though the index draws neither, and that
# is the point: a row of this query and a row of that one are the same object
# (web/endpoint_index.py builds the same EndpointMeasurements the endpoint page
# reads), so a field cannot mean one thing on one page and another on the
# other. Leaving them out would give every index row elapsed_ms None, which
# reads as "the graph carries no elapsed time" for measurements that all carry
# one. They cost 10 ms of the query's 60 ms over the 543-endpoint sweep.
#
# Nothing is substituted into this query: the question is about every endpoint
# the derived graph knows, so there is no ?endpoint to bind.
#
# NO PAGINATION, and no LIMIT. The whole point of the derived
# urn:sparqlwatch:current graph is that it is O(endpoints) rather than
# O(endpoints x history), so this is a flat scan whose cost does not grow as
# sweeps accumulate. Measured on this machine over the 543-endpoint registry
# sweep: 4,344 rows in 34 ms without the newest-run branch and 60 ms with it.
#
# A MEASURED row has ?verdict bound and ?reason unbound; a DECLINED row has
# ?reason bound and ?verdict unbound. They are two different facts and a caller
# must read them as two shapes: "we chose not to run this metric" is not a
# verdict, and rendering a reason where a verdict goes would state a confident
# value for a metric nobody measured. endpoint_measurements.rq's header argues
# this at length and this query inherits it.
#
# WHAT THE METRIC LIST IS. There is no list. ?metric comes back per row from
# what the run recorded, so the columns the page draws are the metrics the
# store holds and not the eight of prober/metrics.toml as it stands today. A
# page built around a fixed eight draws chips no measurement stands behind the
# first time that file changes, and a store holding two runs at different
# metric revisions has two different metric sets in it at once.

PREFIX dqv: <http://www.w3.org/ns/dqv#>
PREFIX prov: <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#>
PREFIX sw: <urn:sparqlwatch:>

SELECT ?endpoint ?run ?generatedAt ?metric ?verdict ?level ?elapsedMs ?reason
       ?declaredCount ?observedCount
       ?emission ?finalised
       ?newestRun ?newestGeneratedAt ?newestEmission ?newestFinalised
       ?newestCompleted ?newestDormant ?newestDormancyReason
WHERE {
  GRAPH sw:current {
    {
      # THE POINTER IS INSIDE EACH UNION ARM, and here that is a measured
      # decision rather than the substitution one endpoint_measurements.rq
      # documents. A triple pattern standing before a braced group in the same
      # block is evaluated with the group and rejoined, and over the
      # 543-endpoint registry sweep this query's 4,344 rows measured:
      #
      #   pointer before the UNION        1,252 ms
      #   pointer inside each arm            30 ms
      #
      # Same solutions either way. The cost of repeating one triple pattern is
      # that it is repeated.
      #
      # It is also what makes the row set the right one: sw:currentRun is the
      # graph's own statement of which endpoints current holds facts for, so
      # binding it here means the page lists exactly those and cannot list an
      # endpoint whose facts were left behind by a load.
      ?endpoint sw:currentRun ?run .
      # rdf:type as a bound variable plus a FILTER, matching exactly the same
      # solutions as "?m a dqv:QualityMeasurement" and costing 0.13 ms rather
      # than 28 ms at thirty runs. The measurement is in
      # endpoint_measurements.rq's header.
      ?measurement dqv:computedOn ?endpoint ;
                   dqv:isMeasurementOf ?metric ;
                   dqv:value ?verdict ;
                   a ?measurementType .
      FILTER (?measurementType = dqv:QualityMeasurement)
      # OPTIONAL because only sw:metric:service-description carries a
      # conformance level today (35 of the sweep's 3,801 measurements), and a
      # measurement from before sw:elapsedMs existed would carry no elapsed
      # time either.
      OPTIONAL { ?measurement sw:level ?level }
      OPTIONAL { ?measurement sw:elapsedMs ?elapsedMs }
      # The two numbers a counting metric compared. The listing shows only the
      # counted one (see _row_size), but both are projected so the index reader
      # and the endpoint reader return the same shape: a row that carried one
      # here and both there would be two dataclasses wearing one name.
      OPTIONAL { ?measurement sw:declaredCount ?declaredCount }
      OPTIONAL { ?measurement sw:observedCount ?observedCount }
    }
    UNION
    {
      # The same pointer, in this arm too. See the arm above.
      ?endpoint sw:currentRun ?run .
      ?notMeasured sw:notMeasuredOn ?endpoint ;
                   sw:notMeasuredMetric ?metric ;
                   sw:notMeasuredReason ?reason ;
                   a ?notMeasuredType .
      FILTER (?notMeasuredType = sw:NotMeasured)
    }
  }
  # ONE HOP to the run current names, for the facts that belong to the run and
  # not to the endpoint: sw:emission and sw:finalised. OPTIONAL with a FILTER
  # that requires what it binds, which means exactly what a plain GRAPH block
  # would mean and is the only form that is flat in the history. The numbers are
  # in endpoint_measurements.rq's header: 151 ms against 0.40 ms at thirty runs.
  #
  # A solution whose run graph is gone is dropped, so an endpoint whose pointer
  # names a vanished run is left out of the index rather than listed with no
  # sweep behind it. That is drift, and web/load_run.py's --check names it.
  OPTIONAL {
    GRAPH ?run {
      ?activity a prov:Activity ;
                prov:generatedAtTime ?generatedAt .
      # Both OPTIONAL, because a run from before stage 1c-b4 carries neither and
      # its verdicts are still the answer to the question that was asked.
      OPTIONAL { ?activity sw:emission ?emission }
      OPTIONAL { ?activity sw:finalised ?finalised }
    }
  }
  FILTER (BOUND(?generatedAt))

  # The newest run in the store, which is routinely not ?run. This is the one
  # selection here that is not per-endpoint, and it is the only way to see the
  # case a per-endpoint question cannot: a run that died partway recorded
  # nothing at all for every endpoint it never reached, so those endpoints'
  # newest facts are an older complete run's and nothing about them hints that
  # tonight's sweep died. On the index that is most of the rows.
  #
  # ITS PLACEMENT IS MEASURED. The aggregate does not mention ?endpoint, so it
  # looks like something that should be evaluated once, at the top of the
  # pattern, and joined. It is not: over the 543-endpoint sweep's 4,344 rows,
  #
  #   this form, the whole branch OPTIONAL          60 ms
  #   the aggregate hoisted above the row scan   1,832 ms
  #   the aggregate below the row scan           1,863 ms
  #
  # because a required join against the aggregate's one row costs the row scan
  # its pointer plan (the 1,252 ms shape above). Asked on its own as a separate
  # query the aggregate is 1.4 ms, so the ~26 ms this branch adds is the price
  # of asking it in the same query as the rows, and it is paid for agreement:
  # endpoint_measurements.rq selects the newest run with this exact subquery, so
  # the index and the endpoint page cannot disagree about which run is newest.
  # A future stage that needs those 100 ms back has the numbers here.
  OPTIONAL {
    {
      SELECT (MAX(?anyGeneratedAt) AS ?newestGeneratedAt) WHERE {
        GRAPH ?anyRun {
          ?anyActivity a prov:Activity ;
                       prov:generatedAtTime ?anyGeneratedAt .
        }
      }
    }
    GRAPH ?newestRun {
      ?newestActivity a prov:Activity ;
                      prov:generatedAtTime ?newestGeneratedAt .
      OPTIONAL { ?newestActivity sw:emission ?newestEmission }
      OPTIONAL { ?newestActivity sw:finalised ?newestFinalised }
    }
  }

  # Whether the newest run recorded finishing THIS row's endpoint, and it is a
  # separate OPTIONAL with a FILTER rather than one nested inside the branch
  # above. THAT IS NOT A STYLE, IT IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN RIGHT AND WRONG
  # ANSWERS, and endpoint_measurements.rq does nest it because there ?endpoint is
  # substituted before evaluation and is therefore a constant inside the group.
  #
  # Here ?endpoint is a variable the rows bind, and an OPTIONAL group is
  # evaluated on its own and then left-joined on the variables it shares with
  # the left. A nested "?newestActivity sw:completedEndpoint ?endpoint" BINDS
  # ?endpoint inside the group, so the group's solutions are exactly the
  # endpoints that run completed, and every row about any OTHER endpoint then has
  # nothing compatible to join with and loses the whole branch. Measured on
  # web/tests/fixtures/run-crashed-partway.nq, which completed one endpoint of
  # three: kadaster got the newest-run facts and the other two came back with
  # ?newestRun unbound, so the two endpoints that newer run never reached were
  # the two the page could not say so about. The condition inverted itself
  # exactly where it mattered.
  #
  # As its OWN OPTIONAL the group still binds ?endpoint, and that is fine and is
  # the point: ?endpoint is shared with the row, so a row whose endpoint the
  # newest run completed joins and a row whose endpoint it did not keeps every
  # other binding it had and leaves ?newestCompleted unbound, which is exactly
  # the fact wanted. What broke above was not binding ?endpoint, it was binding
  # it in the same group as the run-level facts, where losing the join lost
  # those too.
  #
  # Written as a join rather than as a FILTER on a separate variable, which is
  # the same answer and is measured: over the 543-endpoint sweep's 4,344 rows,
  #
  #   this form                                       60 ms
  #   FILTER (?newestCompleted = ?endpoint)        2,373 ms
  #
  # because the filter form has to compare that run's 543 completion markers
  # against every row instead of looking each row's endpoint up.
  OPTIONAL {
    GRAPH ?newestRun { ?newestActivity sw:completedEndpoint ?endpoint }
    BIND (?endpoint AS ?newestCompleted)
  }

  # Whether the newest run declared THIS row's endpoint dormant, and the reason
  # it gave. The other way that run can hold no facts for an endpoint: the
  # marker above says it reached the endpoint, this says it decided not to and
  # published why. Both cannot be true of one endpoint in one run graph, because
  # web/load_run.py refuses a graph that measures an endpoint and declares it
  # dormant.
  #
  # ITS OWN TOP-LEVEL OPTIONAL, beside the marker above and NEVER inside the
  # GRAPH ?newestRun group at the top of this branch. Every word of the twenty
  # five lines above applies to this pattern unchanged, and it is worse here
  # than it is there, because ?newestRun has a SECOND source of bindings: the
  # marker OPTIONAL just above names it too. So a nested dormancy pattern does
  # not leave the newest-run facts unbound, where a caller would see them
  # missing. It leaves them WRONG.
  #
  # Measured, over a store holding run-registry-sample.nq (nine endpoints, the
  # 2026-08-24T19:45:03Z sweep) and the newer run-with-dormancy.nq
  # (2026-08-27T10:00:00Z, which declares one endpoint dormant):
  #
  #   this form   every endpoint    ?newestRun = the 10:00 run, dated, finalised
  #   nested      the dormant one   ?newestRun = the 10:00 run, dated, finalised
  #   nested      the other nine    ?newestRun = the 19:45 run, NO timestamp,
  #                                 finalised False
  #
  # because the nested pattern binds ?endpoint inside the group, narrowing the
  # group's solutions to the endpoints that run declared dormant, and every row
  # about any other endpoint then loses the whole branch and falls through to
  # the marker OPTIONAL, which binds ?newestRun to whatever run completed that
  # endpoint. Each of those nine endpoints is then reported as its own newest
  # run, so newest_run == run, so nothing this query publishes about a newer
  # sweep can fire and the staleness defect goes unfixed for exactly the
  # endpoints it is about. On a store where an endpoint's sw:currentRun and the
  # run that completed it are different runs, two of those rows disagree about
  # ?newestRun and endpoint_measurements.py raises "runs tie as the newest in
  # this store" instead, which is a 500 on the index and on every one of those
  # endpoints' pages.
  #
  # And with no dormancy anywhere in the store the nested form behaves
  # identically, so no fixture written before a dormancy fixture existed can
  # catch any of it. web/queries/endpoint_measurements.rq DOES nest it,
  # correctly, because there ?endpoint is substituted before evaluation and is a
  # constant, so it narrows nothing.
  #
  # WHAT SCOPES THIS TO THE NEWEST RUN is the join on ?newestRun and
  # ?newestActivity, which the branch above binds. A run graph older than the
  # newest one can hold a dormancy declaration for this endpoint (after several
  # weekly skips, several do), and its solution here carries its own ?newestRun,
  # which is incompatible with the row's and is dropped. That is the selection
  # this query makes, deliberately: the newest run's declaration and no other,
  # because an older sweep that declined to ask is this endpoint's history and
  # the sweep that measured it last may be newer than the sweep that last
  # declined. web/tests/fixtures/run-promotes-the-dormant.nq is that shape and
  # test_only_the_newest_runs_declaration_is_read pins the answer.
  #
  # The reason is a nested OPTIONAL inside this one rather than a third
  # top-level one: it is a fact about the declaration, so a row with no
  # declaration must have no reason either, and a run graph is not obliged to
  # carry one.
  #
  # WHAT THIS BRANCH COSTS, measured on this machine over run-registry-sample.nq
  # and run-with-dormancy.nq plus N activity-only run graphs:
  #
  #                       N = 0     N = 100    N = 500
  #   without it         0.9 ms      1.7 ms    10.5 ms
  #   with it            0.8 ms      1.6 ms    11.4 ms
  #
  # 88 rows either way at every depth, which is the number that matters more
  # than the milliseconds: this branch adds no solutions, so it cannot multiply
  # a row set and cannot make two runs look tied as the newest. The growth in
  # both columns belongs to the newest-run aggregate and predates it.
  OPTIONAL {
    GRAPH ?newestRun {
      ?newestActivity sw:dormantEndpoint ?endpoint .
      OPTIONAL { ?endpoint sw:dormancyReason ?newestDormancyReason }
    }
    BIND (?endpoint AS ?newestDormant)
  }
}
